917	/**
918	 * Builds a REAL, Docker-backed `DisposableCleanupTarget` for `project`.
919	 *
920	 * `discoverVolumes` re-resolves the Compose configuration FRESH, at the
921	 * moment cleanup actually runs — "the exact Compose configuration used
922	 * for cleanup" — rather than reusing a value cached from setup time;
923	 * a resolution failure there propagates (fails closed), which
924	 * `cleanupDisposableTargets` reports as this target's own `error`
925	 * without ever attempting a deletion. `externalVolumeKeys` on the
926	 * returned `ownershipContext` is read from a SEPARATE, best-effort
927	 * resolution (degrading to `[]` on failure) — this is only a secondary
928	 * signal for the ownership context; the baseline/project-label checks
929	 * remain the dominant guard, and the PRIMARY fail-closed behavior is
930	 * `discoverVolumes` above.
931	 *
932	 * `env` — the SAME `ComposeEnv` this function receives — is used for
933	 * BOTH halves of the gate/act pair: resolving the configuration
934	 * (`discoverVolumes`'s `resolveConfiguredVolumeNames` and the
935	 * `externalVolumeKeys` lookup above) AND the actual cleanup commands
936	 * (`removeContainersKeepingVolumes`/`removeContainersAndVolumes`
937	 * below). Environment overrides can change which volume names, ports,
938	 * or database targets a Compose project resolves to; a cleanup command
939	 * run under a DIFFERENT environment than the one its own gate just
