44	
45	// Real disposable-persistence, reset, and recreation check for the local
46	// Compose stack's two named volumes (see
47	// docs/architecture/docker-compose-stack.md "Persistence, lifecycle, and
48	// reset"). Proves, against actual running containers and the actual
49	// Docker daemon — never mocks, never a name-prefix guess:
50	//
51	//   1. The REAL named volumes Compose creates, and their ownership
52	//      labels (`com.docker.compose.project` / `.volume`), name only this
53	//      disposable project.
54	//   2. Every byte of persistent data lives in a named volume; no service
55	//      bind-mounts a writable data directory out of the working tree.
56	//   3. The Control DB and the project-test database are distinct, at the
57	//      connection targets docker-compose.yml itself configures, with a
58	//      fixture planted in each and NO leakage in either direction.
59	//   4. A fixture row and a real uploaded object survive a normal
60	//      stop/start (the same containers coming back).
61	//   5. They also survive container REMOVAL and recreation while the
62	//      named volumes are retained (`down` without `--volumes`).
63	//   6. An explicitly scoped reset (`down --volumes`) removes exactly the
64	//      volumes identified in step 1 — and nothing else on the daemon.
65	//   7. The stack recreates afterward from committed configuration alone,
66	//      with the prior row and object genuinely ABSENT.
67	//
68	// A SECOND, independently-named disposable Compose project (`bystander`)
69	// runs its own `postgres` with its own fixture row throughout. It makes
70	// the isolation and reset claims real rather than vacuous: two Compose
71	// projects are shown not to share a data volume, and the reset of the
72	// first project is shown to leave the second one's volume and row
73	// untouched. On a daemon that happens to have nothing else on it, the
74	// "pre-existing volumes survived" check alone would prove nothing.
75	//
76	// Only `postgres` and `storage` are started: they are the only two
77	// services that own persistent data (every other service is stateless —
78	// see the persistence inventory in the doc above). That keeps this check
79	// focused on persistence and avoids rebuilding the three application
80	// images, which tests/integration/docker-compose/stack-smoke.test.ts
81	// already exercises. The documented connection targets are asserted from
82	// the RESOLVED configuration of `migrate-control`/`migrate-project`
83	// rather than by running them (stack-smoke.test.ts already proves those
84	// two exit 0).
85	//
86	// SAFETY — this file performs the only deliberately destructive
87	// operation in the suite, so every guard is explicit:
88	//   - Both Compose project names carry a random suffix
89	//     (`uniqueProjectName`), so neither can be the user's own `relis`
90	//     development stack.
91	//   - BEFORE either project is provisioned: `assertProvisioningSafety`
92	//     validates the RESOLVED configuration (project-scoped volume
93	//     names, no `external: true`, nothing already on the daemon) —
94	//     stopping short of starting a single container if that fails.
95	//   - Before EVERY destructive step (the mid-suite scoped reset AND the
96	//     final cleanup, for BOTH the primary and the bystander project),
97	//     ownership is RE-VERIFIED for the UNION of the exact Compose
98	//     configuration's volume names (re-resolved FRESH, never a cached
99	//     value) and whatever the daemon's own `com.docker.compose.project`
100	//     label filter additionally reports — never label discovery alone,
101	//     which a volume with an incorrect or missing label would escape
102	//     even though `docker compose down --volumes` addresses volumes by
103	//     NAME, not by label (`discoverVolumesRequiringInspection`,
104	//     `evaluateOwnershipGate`/`cleanupDisposableTargets`). A
105	//     configuration-resolution failure FAILS CLOSED. A CONFIRMED
106	//     absence never blocks anything (nothing to delete); an inspection
107	//     FAILURE always does. When ownership cannot be established for a
108	//     volume, deletion is REFUSED and that volume is reported as
109	//     requiring manual inspection; only its project's containers/network
110	//     are removed (always safe), never its data.
111	//   - Every destructive AND volume-preserving cleanup command
112	//     (`cleanupComposeProject`/`composeDown`,
113	//     `removeContainersKeepingVolumesOrThrow`/`composeDownKeepVolumes`)
114	//     runs under the EXACT SAME environment overrides
115	//     (`composeEnv`/`bystanderEnv`) used to resolve the configuration
116	//     its own gate just validated — never a different, possibly-stale
117	//     environment. `createComposeCleanupTarget` threads one `env` value
118	//     through both halves for the final cleanup; the mid-suite reset
119	//     below passes the same `composeEnv` explicitly to its own
120	//     `cleanupComposeProject` call.
121	//   - Every volume present on the daemon BEFORE this test ran is
122	//     captured as a baseline and asserted to still exist afterward.
123	//   - Partial setup (e.g. the bystander project never actually started)
124	//     is handled safely: cleanup only acts on resources the daemon
125	//     confirms belong to a given target, a failure for one target is
126	//     reported without stopping the other, and the ORIGINAL failure
127	//     (whatever made setup fail) is never hidden by a cleanup failure —
128	//     Vitest reports hook failures as additional, separate failures.
129	//   - Deletion is only ever `docker compose -p <that project> down
130	//     --volumes` — never `docker volume rm` by name, never a prune,
131	//     never a filesystem deletion.
132	//   - All published host ports are ephemeral (`0`), so nothing collides
133	//     with a running development stack.
134	//
135	// The NEGATIVE paths of the ownership gate (rejecting a mislabeled,
136	// pre-existing, or externally-declared volume; an inspection FAILURE
137	// never reading as absence; partial-setup cleanup; an unrelated S3 error
138	// never reading as deletion evidence) are deliberately NOT exercised
139	// here against a real volume — doing so would require either genuinely
140	// endangering a resource or an elaborate real-Docker setup that proves
141	// nothing more than a pure function already proves. They are covered
142	// instead, with controlled fakes and no Docker dependency, in
143	// persistence-safety.test.ts.
144	//
145	// Requires the `docker` CLI and a reachable daemon; skips, reporting the
146	// blocker, when absent — never mocks and never fabricates a pass.
147	
148	const here = path.dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));
149	const repoRoot = path.resolve(here, "../../..");
150	const composeFile = path.join(repoRoot, "docker-compose.yml");
151	
152	const POSTGRES_USER = "relis";
153	const CONTROL_DB_NAME = "relis_control";
154	const PROJECT_TEST_DB_NAME = "relis_project_persistence_test";
155	const BYSTANDER_PROJECT_TEST_DB_NAME = "relis_project_bystander_test";
156	
157	/** The services that own persistent data — the only ones this check starts. */
158	const STATEFUL_SERVICES = ["postgres", "storage"];
159	
160	const STORAGE_CREDENTIALS = { accessKey: "relis-dev-access", secretKey: "relis-dev-secret-key-local-only" };
161	
162	/** Every published port ephemeral: neither project may contend with a development stack for a host port. */
163	const EPHEMERAL_PORTS = { SEAWEEDFS_S3_PORT: "0", MAILHOG_UI_PORT: "0", NGINX_HTTP_PORT: "0" };
164	
165	// Computed synchronously at module/collection time — see
166	// isDockerAvailableSync's own doc comment for why an async check in a
167	// beforeAll cannot gate describe.runIf/skipIf correctly.
168	const dockerAvailable = isDockerAvailableSync();
169	
170	if (!dockerAvailable) {
171	  // eslint-disable-next-line no-console
172	  console.warn(
173	    "[docker-compose/persistence.test.ts] SKIPPED: the `docker` CLI is not available (or no daemon is reachable) " +
174	      "in this environment. No real persistence, reset, or recreation behavior was verified; this is a reported " +
175	      "blocker, not a pass.",
176	  );
177	}
178	
179	describe.runIf(dockerAvailable)("disposable local persistence, scoped reset, and clean recreation", () => {
180	  const project: ComposeProject = { projectName: uniqueProjectName("relis-persist"), composeFile, cwd: repoRoot };
181	  const composeEnv = { PROJECT_TEST_DB_NAME, ...EPHEMERAL_PORTS };
182	
183	  /** A second, independent Compose project standing in for another stack on the same daemon. */
184	  const bystander: ComposeProject = { projectName: uniqueProjectName("relis-bystander"), composeFile, cwd: repoRoot };
185	  const bystanderEnv = { PROJECT_TEST_DB_NAME: BYSTANDER_PROJECT_TEST_DB_NAME, ...EPHEMERAL_PORTS };
186	
187	  /** Volumes that already existed on the daemon before this test ran — must all survive it. */
188	  let baselineVolumeNames: string[] = [];
189	  /** The resolved persistence facts (volume names, bind mounts, database targets) for the primary project. */
190	  let resolved: ResolvedComposePersistence;
191	  let bystanderResolved: ResolvedComposePersistence;
192	  /** Recorded once ownership is established, and re-confirmed immediately before the reset. */
193	  let disposableVolumeNames: string[] = [];
194	  /** Container ids from the first startup, to distinguish "restarted" from "recreated". */
195	  let containerIdsAfterFirstStart: Record<string, string[]> = {};
196	
197	  async function psqlIn(target: ComposeProject, database: string, sql: string): Promise<string> {
198	    const result = await composeExec(target, "postgres", ["psql", "-U", POSTGRES_USER, "-d", database, "-tAc", sql]);
199	    expect(result.exitCode, `psql against "${database}" in ${target.projectName} failed: ${result.stderr}`).toBe(0);
200	    return result.stdout.trim();
201	  }
202	
203	  const psql = (database: string, sql: string) => psqlIn(project, database, sql);
204	
205	  /** The object-storage target, re-resolved every time: an ephemeral host port can change across stop/start and recreation. */
206	  async function storageTarget(): Promise<{ endpoint: string; bucket: string; key: string }> {
207	    const port = await composePort(project, "storage", 8333);
208	    return { endpoint: `http://127.0.0.1:${port}`, bucket: OBJECT_FIXTURE.bucket, key: OBJECT_FIXTURE.key };
209	  }
210	
211	  async function startServices(target: ComposeProject, env: Record<string, string>, services: string[]): Promise<void> {
212	    const up = await composeUp(target, env, services, { timeoutMs: 300000 });
213	    if (up.exitCode !== 0) {
214	      throw new Error(
215	        `docker compose -p ${target.projectName} up ${services.join(" ")} failed (exit ${up.exitCode}):\n${up.stdout}\n${up.stderr}`,
216	      );
217	    }
218	    await Promise.all(services.map((service) => waitForHealthy(target, service)));
219	  }
220	
221	  const startStatefulServices = () => startServices(project, composeEnv, STATEFUL_SERVICES);
222	
223	  beforeAll(async () => {
224	    // Baseline FIRST: captured before either project creates anything,
225	    // so the post-reset "nothing unrelated was destroyed" check compares
226	    // against a genuinely untouched snapshot, and so the provisioning
227	    // safety check immediately below has something real to compare
228	    // against.
229	    baselineVolumeNames = await listAllVolumeNames();
230	
231	    [resolved, bystanderResolved] = await Promise.all([
232	      resolveComposePersistence(project, composeEnv),
233	      resolveComposePersistence(bystander, bystanderEnv),
234	    ]);
235	
236	    // Hard safety gate — BEFORE a single container or volume is created
237	    // for EITHER project: the resolved configuration must be genuinely
238	    // disposable (volume names scoped to the project, no
239	    // `external: true`, nothing already on the daemon under these exact
240	    // names). A failure here throws and stops short of provisioning
241	    // anything at all.
242	    assertProvisioningSafety(resolved, baselineVolumeNames);
243	    assertProvisioningSafety(bystanderResolved, baselineVolumeNames);
244	
245	    await startStatefulServices();
246	    containerIdsAfterFirstStart = Object.fromEntries(
247	      await Promise.all(STATEFUL_SERVICES.map(async (service) => [service, await listContainerIds(project, service)] as const)),
248	    );
249	
250	    // The bystander needs only its own PostgreSQL — enough to own a
251	    // separate named volume holding a separate fixture row.
252	    await startServices(bystander, bystanderEnv, ["postgres"]);
253	    await psqlIn(bystander, CONTROL_DB_NAME, createFixtureSql(BYSTANDER_DB_FIXTURE));
254	  }, 600000);
255	
256	  afterAll(async () => {
257	    // Cleans up both projects INDEPENDENTLY and safely, regardless of how
258	    // far setup got. For EACH target, `createComposeCleanupTarget` wires
259	    // up discovery against the UNION of the exact configured volume
260	    // names (re-resolved FRESH here, at cleanup time — never a value
261	    // cached from beforeAll) and whatever the daemon's own project-label
262	    // filter additionally reports — never label discovery alone, which
263	    // a volume with an incorrect or missing label would otherwise
264	    // escape even though `docker compose down --volumes` addresses
265	    // volumes by NAME, not by label. A configuration-resolution failure
266	    // there FAILS CLOSED (propagates as that target's own `error`,
267	    // never as "nothing to check"). Every discovered volume is
268	    // RE-VERIFIED immediately before any deletion — never relying on an
269	    // earlier check — and a failure processing one target never stops
270	    // or skips the other. When ownership cannot be established for a
271	    // target's volume(s), deletion is refused for that target entirely
272	    // (only its containers/network are removed — always safe, since
273	    // they belong solely to its own Compose project) and the exact
274	    // volumes are reported as requiring manual inspection.
275	    const [projectTarget, bystanderTarget] = await Promise.all([
276	      createComposeCleanupTarget(project, composeEnv, baselineVolumeNames),
277	      createComposeCleanupTarget(bystander, bystanderEnv, baselineVolumeNames),
278	    ]);
279	    const outcomes = await cleanupDisposableTargets([projectTarget, bystanderTarget]);
280	
281	    // Verify the cleanup's RESULT rather than trusting it blindly. Every
282	    // problem found here is reported TOGETHER, as an ADDITIONAL failure
283	    // — Vitest records a hook failure on its own, never replacing or
284	    // hiding an earlier test's (or beforeAll's) failure, so the original
285	    // cause always stays visible alongside whatever is found here.
286	    const survivors = new Set(await listAllVolumeNames());
287	    const destroyedPreexisting = baselineVolumeNames.filter((name) => !survivors.has(name));
288	
289	    const problems: string[] = [];
290	    for (const outcome of outcomes) {
291	      if (outcome.error) {
292	        problems.push(`cleanup of "${outcome.label}" failed: ${outcome.error}`);
293	      }
294	      if (outcome.volumesRequiringManualInspection.length > 0) {
295	        problems.push(
296	          `volume(s) for "${outcome.label}" were NOT deleted — ownership could not be established, and they ` +
297	            `require manual inspection: ${outcome.volumesRequiringManualInspection.map((v) => `${v.name} (${v.reason})`).join("; ")}`,
298	        );
299	      }
300	    }
301	    for (const target of [project, bystander]) {
302	      const remaining = await listVolumeNamesByComposeProject(target);
303	      if (remaining.length > 0) {
304	        problems.push(`volumes still labelled for project "${target.projectName}" after cleanup: ${remaining.join(", ")}`);
305	      }
306	    }
307	    if (destroyedPreexisting.length > 0) {
308	      problems.push(`pre-existing volumes no longer present (MUST NOT happen): ${destroyedPreexisting.join(", ")}`);
309	    }
310	
311	    if (problems.length > 0) {
312	      throw new Error(`Cleanup did not complete correctly:\n- ${problems.join("\n- ")}`);
313	    }
314	  }, 420000);
315	
316	  it("creates exactly two project-scoped named volumes, owned by this disposable project and nothing else", async () => {
317	    // The volume keys declared in docker-compose.yml, resolved to the
318	    // real Docker volume names Compose will create. Project-prefixed by
319	    // Compose itself — which is what keeps two Compose projects from
320	    // sharing a data volume.
321	    expect(Object.keys(resolved.volumeNames).sort()).toEqual(["postgres-data", "storage-data"]);
322	    expect(resolved.volumeNames["postgres-data"]).toBe(`${project.projectName}_postgres-data`);
323	    expect(resolved.volumeNames["storage-data"]).toBe(`${project.projectName}_storage-data`);
324	
325	    // No volume may be `external: true`: that would point at a
326	    // pre-existing user-owned resource this test must never delete.
327	    expect(resolved.externalVolumes).toEqual([]);
328	
329	    // This project is genuinely disposable, not the default development
330	    // stack whose volumes would be `relis_*`.
331	    expect(project.projectName).toMatch(/^relis-persist-[0-9a-f]{8}$/);
332	    expect(project.projectName).not.toBe("relis");
333	
334	    // Ground truth from the DAEMON: which volumes it reports as owned by
335	    // this project, via Compose's own labels — not a name-prefix guess.
336	    disposableVolumeNames = (await listVolumeNamesByComposeProject(project)).sort();
337	    expect(disposableVolumeNames).toEqual([resolved.volumeNames["postgres-data"], resolved.volumeNames["storage-data"]].sort());
338	
339	    for (const [volumeKey, volumeName] of Object.entries(resolved.volumeNames)) {
340	      const ownership = await inspectVolume(volumeName);
341	      expect(ownership, `volume ${volumeName} should exist`).toBeDefined();
