207	 * Runs `service`'s command in a NEW, disposable container (not the
208	 * long-running one `composeUp` may have already started), with `env`
209	 * overrides applied directly to that one invocation — e.g. to simulate a
210	 * genuinely missing or invalid required variable without touching the
211	 * project's other services. Mirrors `docker compose run --rm -e ...`.
212	 *
213	 * `-T` disables pseudo-TTY allocation — without it, `docker compose run`
214	 * can allocate one by default, which merges the container's stdout and
215	 * stderr into a single stream; callers that need to distinguish which
216	 * stream a diagnostic was written to (e.g. a ConfigValidationError,
217	 * logged via console.error to stderr — see config-validation.test.ts)
218	 * require them to stay separate.
219	 *
220	 * TWO DISTINCT environments are in play here, and this function must not
221	 * conflate them:
222	 *   - `env` (this parameter) becomes `-e KEY=VALUE` flags, which set/
223	 *     override variables INSIDE the one-off container `run` creates —
224	 *     AFTER Compose has already resolved the service's own config. This
225	 *     is what lets a caller simulate "this one container sees a missing
226	 *     or invalid value" without touching anything else.
227	 *   - `options.composeEnv` is the COMPOSE INTERPOLATION environment —
228	 *     what the `docker compose` CLI subprocess itself sees, which is what
229	 *     Compose uses to resolve `${VAR}`/`${VAR:-default}` substitutions
230	 *     when it builds the target service's config in the first place
231	 *     (e.g. `migrate-control`'s own `CONTROL_DATABASE_URL:
232	 *     postgresql://${POSTGRES_USER:-relis}:${POSTGRES_PASSWORD:-changeme}@postgres:5432/${POSTGRES_DB:-relis_control}`).
233	 *     Previously this function passed no explicit env to that subprocess
234	 *     at all, so this interpolation silently fell back to whatever
235	 *     ambient/default `POSTGRES_*` values happened to be present —
236	 *     independent of, and potentially DIFFERENT from, the values a
237	 *     caller used to actually provision PostgreSQL (e.g. via
238	 *     `composeUp`), producing a baked-in `CONTROL_DATABASE_URL` with the
239	 *     WRONG credentials/database name even though the right ones were
240	 *     passed as `-e` overrides.
241	 *
242	 * When `options.composeEnv` is omitted, `env` is reused for the
243	 * interpolation environment too — this preserves every existing caller's
244	 * behavior unchanged (none of them reference a host-interpolated Compose
245	 * variable through `env` today; they only set container-level overrides
246	 * that are not themselves `${...}`-substituted anywhere in
247	 * docker-compose.yml). Callers that provision PostgreSQL with explicit
248	 * `POSTGRES_USER`/`POSTGRES_PASSWORD`/`POSTGRES_DB`/`PROJECT_TEST_DB_NAME`
249	 * values (as `composeUp` was already called with) MUST pass the SAME
250	 * object as `options.composeEnv` here too, or the service's own
251	 * interpolated config will silently diverge from what was actually
252	 * provisioned.
253	 */
